  "preamble": "Diphthongization of the close front vowel [i] (here long [iː]) to a diphthong with a back rounded onset, [ʊi]. It is characteristic of Faroese, where Old Norse í and ý, after an early breaking of the long close vowels, merged as /ʊi/, still written í and ý (Old Norse íss > Faroese ísur /ˈʊiːsʊɹ/ 'ice').",
  "phoneticEffects": "Diphthongization, Backing, Rounding",
